// Uppercase standard input — the first program to compose two stream transforms, and the reason
// the flattening question is not settled.
//
// `@/packages/stream` was the first rewrite in this directory. Its whole argument is that a
// transform *is* a stream, so transforms compose: *"Composition is `upperCase(scalars(src))` rather
// than two closures wired to each other by whoever owns the loop."* Nothing in the directory has
// ever written that composition — measured 2026-09-04, `stream` is one of 26 vision packages
// imported by nothing.
//
// This is that program. The single stage works. The **second** stage is the finding.

import { Result, Bytes, AsyncGenerator } from "core";
import { In, Out, NotGranted } from "std";
import { upperCase, NotText } from "@/packages/stream";

/** One stage: bytes in, uppercase bytes out. */
export async i32 upper(In in, Out out) {
  try await for (Bytes chunk in upperCase(in.stream())) { _ = out.write(chunk); }
  return 0;
}

// ## The second stage, and what its type is
//
// `upperCase` is
//
//     async gen<u8[]> Result<void, union<E, NotText>> upperCase<E>(
//       AsyncGenerator<u8[], Result<void, E>> src)
//
// so it takes a stream failing with `E` and answers one failing with `union<E, NotText>` — *"the
// source's error set plus the one thing this code can be wrong about"*, which reads exactly right
// for one stage. Stack two:
//
//     upperCase(upperCase(in.stream()))
//
//   * the inner one has `E = NotGranted`, so it answers `union<NotGranted, NotText>`;
//   * the outer one's `E` is *that*, so it answers `union<union<NotGranted, NotText>, NotText>`.
//
// **`NotText` is in there twice, at two depths.** Under the nesting lowering
// `../../../TECHNICAL.md` measures — a union is an enum whose variant may hold an enum — those are
// two different variants of two different enums, and `Err(is NotText):` matches the outer one only.
// A caller asking *was the input not text* gets the right answer when the second stage found it and
// the wrong answer when the first did.
//
// ## Which means the flattening question has two consumers wanting opposite things
//
// `TECHNICAL.md` says nesting *"is the one that preserves the grouping, and it is also the simpler
// one to implement"*, and cites `@/packages/box/src/gunzip.wac`: `union<SourceFailed, Corrupt>`
// where `Corrupt` is a union of eight, and `Err(is Corrupt):` is one arm for the group. That is
// right and it depends on the members being **disjoint**.
//
// A stacked transform is the case where they are not. Flattening gives
// `union<NotGranted, NotText>` — one `NotText`, `Err(is NotText)` meaning what it says, and the
// depth gone. Nesting gives the group and the duplicate.
//
// So the two are not a preference. **gunzip needs nesting and this needs flattening**, and neither
// is a corner case: grouping a family of faults and stacking a pipeline are the two things error
// unions are for. `../../../QUESTIONS.md`.
//
// ## What could not be written
//
// **The two-stage program itself.** Not because the types are wrong — they compose and the
// signature is honest — but because there is nothing to say about a `NotText` from the first stage
// that is different from one from the second, so the program that shows the problem is also the
// program nobody would write. Uppercasing twice is idempotent. The real shape is
// `upperCase(gunzip(src))`, where the inner failure set is eight members and the outer adds one, and
// **that** is worth writing the day `@/packages/gzip`'s generator and this one can be named in one
// file — which needs the overlay question answered first.
//
// **`in.stream()`'s failure is `NotGranted` and nothing else.** So the single-stage program above
// has `union<NotGranted, NotText>`, and a caller distinguishing them is distinguishing *the
// capability was not granted* from *the bytes were not text* — which is right, and is the one place
// this file can show the union earning its keep rather than duplicating.
